import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The otel package: setting it up, every request timed by its route, the app's own counters,
## histograms and gauges, where they are sent, the server's JSON log for a collector, how it works,
## and what it doesn't do yet.
## Static: a collector is a server.
meta = {
	title: "OpenTelemetry",
	description: "The otel package for Roc Solid apps: metrics sent over OTLP to any OpenTelemetry Collector or backend, every request timed by its route, the app's own counters, histograms and gauges, and the server's JSON log for a collector to read.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "setup", label: "Setting up" },
		{ id: "requests", label: "Every request, timed" },
		{ id: "instruments", label: "Measuring your own" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where they go" },
		{ id: "logs", label: "Logs" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/otel</code>]}
				title="Metrics and logs, for any OpenTelemetry backend"
			>
				Time every request by its route, count what your app cares about, and send it over OTLP to an
				OpenTelemetry Collector or a backend that accepts it (Grafana Cloud, Honeycomb, Datadog, and
				others). For logs, a collector reads the server's JSON log; the app adds nothing.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				OpenTelemetry is the standard most monitoring tools accept. The
				<a href={Paths.examples_uptime}>uptime example</a> uses this package: a histogram of each check's
				latency, and a gauge of whether each monitor is up.
			</p>

			<Heading id="setup">Setting up</Heading>
			<ol>
				<li>Name the package in the app's header: <code>otel: "…/packages/otel/main.roc"</code>.</li>
				<li>
					List its plugin, <code>Otel.plugin({"{}"})</code>, in the program's <code>plugins</code>, and
					<code>import otel.Otel</code> where you record. Nothing is copied into the app.
				</li>
				<li>
					Put <code>Otel.middleware({"{}"})</code> first in the app's
					<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a>, <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>, so it
					times the rest too.
				</li>
				<li>
					Start the server with <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> set to your collector, such as
					<code>http://localhost:4318</code>. In development, put it in the app's <code>.env</code>, which
					<code>rocx dev</code> reads.
				</li>
			</ol>

			<Heading id="requests">Every request, timed</Heading>
			<Code
				title="main.roc, in part"
				source={"# main.roc\nprogram = App.program({\n\tinit!: |started| Ok({ facts: started }),\n\tplugins: [Otel.plugin({})],\n})\n\n# routes/+middleware.roc: around every request, first, so it times the rest too\nmiddleware = Otel.middleware({}).then(Middlewares.default)"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>Otel.middleware({"{}"})</code> times every request through it as
				<code>http.server.request.duration</code>, a histogram in seconds, with the attributes of
				OpenTelemetry's HTTP conventions:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>http.request.method</code>, or <code>_OTHER</code> for a method HTTP doesn't define;</li>
				<li>
					<code>http.route</code>, the route's pattern (<code>/posts/[id]</code>, never
					<code>/posts/42</code>, so each post doesn't get a series of its own), left out when no route
					matched;
				</li>
				<li><code>http.response.status_code</code>.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="instruments">Measuring your own</Heading>
			<p>
				An instrument is something your app measures. Define one with <code>Metric</code>, and record values
				with <code>Otel.record!</code> anywhere on the server, with attributes that label each value:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Anywhere on the server"
				source={"signups = Metric.counter({ name: \"app.signups\", description: \"Members who signed up.\", unit: \"{member}\" })\nOtel.record!(signups, 1, [(\"plan\", \"pro\")])\n\ncheckout = Metric.histogram({ name: \"app.checkout.duration\", description: \"Checkout.\", unit: \"s\", buckets: Metric.seconds })\nOtel.record!(checkout, seconds, [(\"method\", \"card\")])\n\nup = Metric.gauge({ name: \"app.service.up\", description: \"Whether the service answered (1) or not (0).\", unit: \"1\" })\nOtel.record!(up, 1, [(\"service\", \"payments\")])"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					A <strong>counter</strong> adds up what it is given (signups, emails sent). It never goes down: a
					negative value is dropped.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong>histogram</strong> counts values into buckets and sums them (how long a checkout took).
					<code>Metric.seconds</code> is OpenTelemetry's buckets for durations, from 5 ms to 10 s.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong>gauge</strong> keeps the last value set, negative too (whether a service is up, how many
					jobs wait), and is sent at each export until set again.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Each combination of attribute values is a separate series, so keep the values few: a user's id or a
				full URL would make a series per user or URL. An instrument keeps at most 2000 series and drops new
				ones past that. A name is one kind of instrument: a value recorded under a name another kind uses is
				dropped.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where they go</Heading>
			<p>
				It reads the standard environment variables, as every OpenTelemetry SDK does:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What it sets</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>the collector's address; <code>/v1/metrics</code> is added</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code></td><td>headers to send, <code>name=value,…</code>, such as a backend's key</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_SERVICE_NAME</code>, <code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code></td><td>the service's name and attributes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_INTERVAL</code></td><td>milliseconds between sends (60000)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true</code></td><td>turns it off</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				With no endpoint, metrics are off: nothing is measured, kept or sent, so it costs nothing while you
				develop. The package's README lists the other variables it reads (a metrics-only endpoint and
				headers, the send timeout, <code>OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER=none</code>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="logs">Logs</Heading>
			<p>
				Logs need nothing from this package. The server's <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#logging"}>log</a>
				is JSON, one object a line (the platform's and the app's alike), when stderr isn't a terminal (as
				where logs are collected), with <code>ROCX_LOG=json</code>, or when the program says
				<code>{"logs: { format: Json }"}</code>. The Collector's <code>filelog</code> receiver turns each line
				into a log record: time, level as severity, message as body, and fields as attributes. The package's
				README has the collector's configuration.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				Recording a value puts it in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>), so a request
				never waits. Each second, the plugin's loop adds what is there to its sums; every export interval,
				and once more as the server stops, it sends them all in one request (OTLP over HTTP, in JSON).
			</p>
			<p>
				The sums are cumulative, since the loop started, so a failed send loses nothing: the next carries it
				all, and the failure is logged as a warning. Nothing goes to the database: a restart starts the sums
				again with a new start time, which a collector reads as a reset counter.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Traces.</strong> They need to know which request a query or fetch runs for without the app
					passing it by hand; Roc has no such context yet.
				</li>
				<li>Up-down counters, exemplars, and Prometheus's <code>/metrics</code> for pulling.</li>
				<li>A job's duration and outcome.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
